\pard \fs24\s1\fi-720\li720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlblt\pnstart1\pnindent1\pnhang{\pntxta {\'95}}}

{\plain \fs24 }{\plain \fs24 \i Comparing Comparisons and {\'91}Comparative Method{\'92}\par
}\pard \fs24\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 \i \tab }{\plain \fs24 The terms, {\u8220\'93}compare{\u8221\'94}, {\u8220\'93}comparison{\u8221\'94}, {\u8220\'93}comparative{\u8221\'94}, {\u8220\'93}comparative method{\u8221\'94} and their \softline
semantic kin are widely used in informal, everyday, non-technical language and apply to a wide \softline
range of referents. In this discussion, I want to concentrate instead on a rather formal and more \softline
technically relevant span of such usages, and to the referents to which terms such as these may be \softline
applied. A first order of business is then to do some conceptual tidying so that the technical or \softline
formal sense of comparison can be isolated for study. I want therefore to exclude a number of \softline
logically {\u8216\'91}uninteresting{\u8217\'92} or informal uses of these terms so that we can concentrate on the usages \softline
and referents that spawn the most acute controversy. \par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab The first everyday sense of comparison to be to aside is the simple sense of comparing as}{\plain \fs24 \ul  \softline
pairing, matching or laying side by side}{\plain \fs24 . From the Latin {\u8216\'91}}{\plain \fs24 \ul comparare}{\plain \fs24 ,{\u8217\'92} the English {\u8216\'91}compare{\u8217\'92} \softline
literally means to {\u8216\'91}match{\u8217\'92} or to {\u8216\'91}pair.{\u8217\'92} I take it as obvious that in the study of religion much more \softline
is at stake than the neutral idea of laying things out in pairs. In the study of religion, for example,  \softline
we debate the hows and whys of the comparison and comparative study of religions. We are, or \softline
ought to be, sensitive, to the implications of what it is that we are really doing and assuming in \softline
comparing religions. Thus when, say, two religions are }{\plain \fs24 \i paired}{\plain \fs24 , it is the purpose behind or \softline
appropriateness of such pairing that often lurks in the background. \par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab Second, in formal contexts of the comparative study of religions, I shall want to put \softline
severe limits on the exclusive understanding of comparing as }{\plain \fs24 \ul likening}{\plain \fs24  as well. Although, as we \softline
will see, underlying purposes }{\plain \fs24 \ul may}{\plain \fs24  be concealed in mere pairing, they need not necessarily be \softline
intended. Thus, I recognize that it is natural enough to assume that certain hidden agendas may \softline
be implied in such simple informal comparisons as pairings because at least one part of the \softline
ordinary, informal meaning of {\u8216\'91}compare{\u8217\'92} suggests as much {\u8211\'96} to compare is commonly thought to \softline
{\u8216\'91}liken,{\u8217\'92} to suggest similarity, analogy, resemblance or equality between those things }{\plain \fs24 \i compared}{\plain \fs24 .  \softline
Here {\u8216\'91}to compare{\u8217\'92} contrasts to {\u8216\'91}to contrast{\u8217\'92} (sic) as in the fusty (and redundant) language of the \softline
quiz, test and examination, where we ask students to {\u8216\'91}compare and contrast{\u8217\'92} this or that. But this \softline
usage is redundant, as I think honest reflection will reveal. We dare not risk students thinking \softline
that they are only being asked to cite }{\plain \fs24 \ul likenesses}{\plain \fs24 , and not also }{\plain \fs24 \ul differences}{\plain \fs24 . So, we become brutally \softline
explicit and underline the fact that we seek a rounded answer to the question to hand by having \softline
differences cited as well as similarities. \par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab More seriously however, especially in the study of religion, the implication of likeness in \softline
the vulgar language of comparison is also why some religious folk have stood against religious \softline
studies or comparative study of religions. They object even to the most innocent pairing {\u8211\'96} \softline
comparison  {\u8211\'96} of one religion to another because they believe their religion unique {\u8211\'96} and thus \softline
}{\plain \fs24 \i incomparable}{\plain \fs24 . They take this very act of {\u8216\'91}com-pairing{\u8217\'92} to imply the }{\plain \fs24 \ul religious equality}{\plain \fs24  of the \softline
religions in question {\u8211\'96} or at the very least }{\plain \fs24 \i equality}{\plain \fs24  insofar as their common membership in a class \softline
that includes both religions are members. To some, even the mere}{\plain \fs24 \i  implication}{\plain \fs24  of common class \softline
membership diminishes the unique value of their religion in the process. Such scruples were \softline
precisely why one of the first phenomenologists of religion, the pious Christian, Chantepie de la \softline
Saussaye, omitted Christianity from his pioneering }{\plain \fs24 \ul Manual of the History of \softline
Religions}{\plain \fs24 .(Chantepie de la Saussaye 1897; Hubert 1904) Doubtless as well Karl Barth{\u8217\'92}s 
distinction between the Word and the {\u8216\'91}religion{\u8217\'92} of Christianity rests on the same foundations.  \par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab A third sense of comparison rises above the merely everyday and informal, and fulfils a \softline
very useful function for creative thinking. This is comparison in the sense of achieving a \softline
{\u8216\'91}comparative perspective.{\u8217\'92} When things are paired there may not be any agendas or purposes in \softline
mind. All sorts of agendas, or none at all, may flow simply from things merely being put into \softline
}{\plain \fs24 \i {\u8216\'91}comparative perspective}{\plain \fs24 {\u8217\'92} {\u8211\'96} from their being laid side by side, with the suggestion that they }{\plain \fs24 \ul may}{\plain \fs24  \softline
belong to a common class, even if not yet determined. Rather than offering threats, I think that \softline
some of these instances of such pairings offer benefits to creative thinking. The mere suggestion \softline
of common class membership may awaken thought, even when no particular purpose for doing \softline
so may be in mind, or when no particular hypothesis is being tested. This is altogether salutary \softline
for stimulating thinking, and although it may not be comparison at its most powerful, it is useful \softline
all the same. For example, while one may pair the engagement of the United States in Iraq with \softline
other foreign military engagements, some may be more suggestive than others. Is it, for example, \softline
to be put into the same class as that of the USA in Viet Nam, or the French in Algeria, or the \softline
Israelis in southern Lebanon? Each pairing carries with it a whole series of interesting \softline
implications, extended analogies or differences, and further openings for thought without \softline
committing one to a particular conclusion or hypothesis. Thus a}{\plain \fs24 \i  comparative perspective }{\plain \fs24 can be a \softline
delightfully }{\plain \fs24 \i heuristic}{\plain \fs24 , because comparison can help us think {\u8211\'96} without necessarily determining \softline
the direction or end-result of such thinking. As I mentioned, it draws somewhat shy of meriting \softline
being an element in a {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative studies because it is not necessarily tied \softline
to advancing any sort of hypothesis, even if it may provide an opening so to do.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \par
}{\pntext\pard {\'95}\tab}
\pard \fs24\s1\fi-720\li720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlblt\pnstart1\pnindent1\pnhang{\pntxta {\'95}}}

{\plain \fs24 }{\plain \fs24 \i A Classic Revisited: {\'93}Comparative Method{\'94} }{\plain \fs24 \par
}\pard \fs24\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 \tab Fourth, and finally, in sorting through common usages of terms having to do with \softline
comparison, the famous {\u8220\'93}comparative method{\u8221\'94} of 19}{\plain \fs24 \super th}{\plain \fs24  century anthropology should be sorted out \softline
from other uses. Suffice it to say then that it is problematic how {\u8220\'93}strong{\u8221\'94} a program of \softline
comparative studies {\u8220\'93}comparative method{\u8221\'94} actually is. Evans-Pritchard, for example,  brushed \softline
off {\u8220\'93}comparative method{\u8221\'94} by observing that it has no particular method to it and has been \softline
practiced informally, and thus innocuously, since Aristotle. It is a {\u8220\'93}title which means little more \softline
than if one wishes to make a general statement about the nature of some institution, one has first \softline
to examine it in a number of different societies.{\u8221\'94}(Evans-Pritchard 1981: p. 173) When Frazer, \softline
Tylor or Eliade, for example,  are }{\plain \fs24 \ul not}{\plain \fs24  cryptically advancing a thesis, the heaping up of apparent \softline
likenesses amounts to no more than this. Such use of comparison might be characterized as just \softline
}{\plain \fs24 \ul pairing}{\plain \fs24  items or at the very best, adding a{\u8220\'93}comparative perspective{\u8221\'94} of no necessary value. No \softline
logical necessity lies behind these instances of comparison, or even less a material necessity {\u8211\'96} at \softline
least not until the }{\plain \fs24 \ul grounds}{\plain \fs24  of such a catalogue of pairings is made clear or discovered.  In this \softline
vein, such sorts of comparative studies would be said at best to be {\u8220\'93}useful{\u8221\'94}, {\u8220\'93}illustrative{\u8221\'94}, \softline
{\u8220\'93}provocative{\u8221\'94}, {\u8220\'93}interesting{\u8221\'94} and so on in a rather patronizing litany of qualifiers.  Comparative \softline
studies would then not be }{\plain \fs24 \ul necessary}{\plain \fs24  for understanding; they would merely be extras in an already \softline
self-sufficient particular study.  At best they provide {\u8220\'93}analogies{\u8221\'94}, which are optional creations of \softline
the whims and/or intellectual virtuosity of investigators. In the case then where }{\plain \fs24 \ul no hidden}{\plain \fs24  agenda \softline
is present, the kind of comparison offered in marshaling such lists of analogies would be \softline
decidedly {\u8220\'93}weak.{\u8221\'94}\par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab In the cases, however, where hypotheses are being put forth, and in their own way, tested, 
{\u8220\'93}comparative method{\u8221\'94} shows itself to be an early example of a  {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative \softline
studies.{\u8221\'94} Tylorian {\u8220\'93}comparative method{\u8221\'94} would, for instance,  count as just such a {\u8220\'93}strong \softline
program{\u8221\'94} {\u8211\'96}  {\u8220\'93}rigorous,{\u8221\'94} Evan-Pritchard called it {\u8211\'96} of comparative studies of its time.\par
}{\plain \fs24 (Evans-Pritchard 1981: p. 173)  If its universal claims regarding the nature of human \softline
evolutionary schemes of development and a particular society{\u8217\'92}s place in it were true, and if all \softline
societies moved through the same objective evolutionary levels of development, then the \softline
analogies Tylor offered would have been rooted in the nature of things. They were true because \softline
he thought there was a material }{\plain \fs24 \ul necessity}{\plain \fs24  in which the cultural differences seen round the world \softline
participated. Veneration of spirits, no matter what various names they bore in different cultures, \softline
was still the property of a particular level of universal evolutionary development. The historical \softline
classification of stages of this developmental movement and the place a particular society \softline
occupied in it, were held to be }{\plain \fs24 \ul objectively}{\plain \fs24  true.  Societies, cultures and their contents were \softline
natural {\u8220\'93}phenomena{\u8221\'94}; they were {\u8220\'93}there{\u8221\'94} in the same sense animal species and their features were \softline
{\u8220\'93}there.{\u8221\'94}  Small wonder then that when the arguments of Boas against cultural evolution gained \softline
strength that the fortunes of the {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} of comparative studies declined.  Resting as it \softline
did on evolutionary bases, }{\plain \fs24 \ul the}{\plain \fs24  {\u8220\'93}comparative method{\u8221\'94} as a {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} would either have to \softline
be abandoned or re-invented.  The Boasians chose abandon it. As we will see, the Durkheimians \softline
sought to re-invent it.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab When {\u8220\'93}comparative method{\u8221\'94} shows the kind of {\u8216\'91}strength{\u8217\'92} of which it is more than \softline
capable, religious folk resisting comparison have a good deal to fear from it. Ninian Smart often  \softline
would joke, half seriously that {\u8220\'93} comparative study of religion tends to make one comparatively \softline
religious.{\u8221\'94} }{\plain \fs24  Using this particular kind of approach to comparison, many pioneers in the field of \softline
comparative study of religions indeed sought {\u8211\'96} overtly or cryptically {\u8211\'96} to support certain \softline
subversive theses about the general nature of religion, and ultimately against Christianity in \softline
particular. Sir James George Frazer, Edward Burnett Tylor and other British anthropologists of \softline
the day practiced what they called, somewhat imperialistically, }{\plain \fs24 \ul the}{\plain \fs24  {\u8220\'93}comparative method.{\u8221\'94}  \softline
Frazer, for instance  clearly sought to argue {\u8211\'96} at time implicitly, at times explicitly {\u8211\'96} that there \softline
were significant and substantial parallels between so-called {\u8220\'93}pagan{\u8221\'94} myths and Christian motifs. \softline
For Frazer, this meant that the vaunted uniqueness of Christianity was simply false.  Frazer{\u8217\'92}s \softline
arguments for the existence of {\u8220\'93}Pagan Christs{\u8221\'94} in the Mediterranean world, for example, was  not \softline
meant to argue for some apologetically satisfying }{\plain \fs24 \i preparatio evangelium}{\plain \fs24 , but rather for the thesis \softline
that Christianity had an ultimately {\u8216\'91}pagan{\u8217\'92} nature, because its historical and cultural roots were \softline
{\u8220\'93}pagan.{\u8221\'94}  For Frazer and other 19}{\plain \fs24 \super th}{\plain \fs24  century evolutionists, this condemned religion (and \softline
Christianity in particular) as having a {\u8216\'91}primitive{\u8217\'92} character {\u8211\'96} a characterization for an evolutionist \softline
like Frazer that put Christianity {\u8216\'91}in its place{\u8217\'92} as a crude and uncivilized thing. On top of this, \softline
Christianity{\u8217\'92}s claim to trans-historical revealed truth were therefore laid bare as bogus, since key \softline
imagery of the Christian tradition could be found in the pagan religions preceding Christianity! \softline
Indeed, pagan religions shaped the very spirituality of Christianity, such as, for example, the ideal \softline
of the resurrection foreshadowed in Mediterranean images of the dying and rising god. As a \softline
latecomer on historical scene, Christianity had borrowed freely from these base and {\u8216\'91}primitive{\u8217\'92} \softline
non-Christian sources, Frazer and others alleged.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab Ironically, one of the greatest defenders of the integrity and value of religion, including \softline
Christianity, Mircea Eliade, employed the same logic of {\u8220\'93}comparative method{\u8221\'94}  but turned its \softline
implications on its head, so to speak. As a classic anti-evolutionist, Eliade saw the {\u8216\'91}primitive{\u8217\'92} {\u8211\'96} 
{\u8220\'93}archaic religious{\u8221\'94} humanity {\u8211\'96} as anything but crude and base. Indeed, from Eliade{\u8217\'92}s perspective \softline
archaic religious consciousness was the supreme form of religious consciousness, and from it all \softline
subsequent forms of spirituality evidence a definite }{\plain \fs24 \ul de-evolutionary}{\plain \fs24 \~decline. The struggle of the \softline
moderns is precisely to try to recapture and live the archaic vision, after having degenerated for \softline
many years in terms of their spirituality. By contrast, therefore, Eliade{\u8217\'92}s }{\plain \fs24 \i comparisons}{\plain \fs24  {\u8211\'96} his \softline
citation of likenesses between Christian and {\u8216\'91}pagan{\u8217\'92} images was meant to ground Christian \softline
spirituality in some trans-historic or {\u8216\'91}}{\plain \fs24 \ul ab- original{\u8217\'92}}{\plain \fs24  {\u8220\'93}archaic{\u8221\'94} religiosity of great value. That, for \softline
instance, the {\u8216\'91}tree{\u8217\'92} of  the crucifixion could be compared (likened) to the archaic, {\u8216\'91}ab-original{\u8217\'92}, \softline
archetypal World Tree or the tree from which the {\u8216\'91}pagan{\u8217\'92} deity, Attis, was hung was only further \softline
evidence that Christianity was rooted in an archaic spiritual experience.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab Comparison, however cryptically used by Eliade for the purposes of testing and finally \softline
proving such hypotheses to be true, fell into line with the methods of the 19}{\plain \fs24 \super th}{\plain \fs24  century evolutionist \softline
enemies of religion. At its best, Christianity succeeds in perpetuating archaic religiosity, despite \softline
claims to Christian uniqueness made by the {\u8216\'91}orthodox.{\u8217\'92}  And, thus for Eliade, comparison had a \softline
kind of force {\u8211\'96} {\u8220\'93}strength{\u8221\'94} {\u8211\'96} that informal {\u8211\'96} {\u8220\'93}weak{\u8221\'94}  {\u8211\'96} comparisons, such as mere pairing or even \softline
an intellectually playful setting into{\u8216\'91}comparative perspective,{\u8217\'92} could never achieve.  This, albeit \softline
typically unemphatic, use of comparison by Eliade is one reason, one might suggest, for the force \softline
of his influence. However shrouded in obscure language were his plans and purposes in making \softline
comparisons, Eliade was putting forth {\u8216\'91}strong{\u8217\'92} and consequential {\u8211\'96} if, in my view, false {\u8211\'96} }{\plain \fs24 \ul theses}{\plain \fs24  \softline
about the nature of religion.  The {\u8220\'93}comparative method{\u8221\'94} of the 19}{\plain \fs24 \super th}{\plain \fs24  century anthropologists and \softline
their unlikely heirs, such as Eliade, is then what we can call a {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative \softline
studies. My argument lies with this style of comparison, although it is not the only kind of \softline
{\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} that one might construct today  {\u8211\'96} all points to which I shall return shortly.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab Let me draw some preliminary conclusions about formal or technical uses of comparison \softline
in study of religion. First, while one may recognize that in common usage comparison tends to \softline
imply }{\plain \fs24 \i similarity}{\plain \fs24 , it need not. At the very least, all we mean in comparing things is to bring things \softline
into relation with one another {\u8211\'96} to {\u8216\'91}pair{\u8217\'92} them for whatever purpose one may have. The purposes \softline
may be apologetic or not. They may be neither as well. And, here we can celebrate the \softline
intellectual utility of putting things into comparative perspective {\u8211\'96} without at the same time \softline
committing ourselves to hard claims about the ontology of things compared. To lay things \softline
alongside one another need not mean that we are declaring them of the same substance {\u8211\'96} pious \softline
Christians can still compare Christianity with other religions without feeling that the essential \softline
uniqueness of Christianity is jeopardized.  And, although the pioneers of comparative studies in \softline
the 19}{\plain \fs24 \super th}{\plain \fs24  century attributed to themselves the ownership of comparative studies by declaring what \softline
they did to be the {\u8220\'93}comparative method,{\u8221\'94} we need not bow to their authority, even as we may \softline
admire their ambitions to use comparison in a powerful way to make statements and put \softline
hypotheses up to the test. As we will see, there are other ways for comparative studies to be \softline
{\u8220\'93}strong{\u8221\'94} than by tying it to an evolutionist (or devolutionist in Eliade{\u8217\'92}s case) view of human \softline
culture.\par
