{\plain \fs24 \tab As much a part of the study of religions as both informal and partially formal approaches \softline
to comparison have been, if we seek a study of religion that at least approaches something of a \softline
{\u8216\'91}scientific{\u8217\'92} character I think we would need to articulate a  {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative 
studies {\u8211\'96} and one that does not simply reprise what the 19}{\plain \fs24 \super th}{\plain \fs24  century comparativists did. By \softline
{\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} I understand that comparisons are devised for the sake of testing hypotheses {\u8211\'96} \softline
as the 19}{\plain \fs24 \super th}{\plain \fs24  century evolutionists did, although often less than frankly. Further, the {\u8220\'93}strength{\u8221\'94} of a \softline
comparison lies as well in whether the act of comparing {\u8211\'96} the act of setting some particular \softline
within a presumed }{\plain \fs24 \ul class}{\plain \fs24  is either logically, conceptually, or in fact, }{\plain \fs24 \ul necessary}{\plain \fs24  for understanding \softline
the particular {\u8211\'96} and in a sense which might be said to constitute {\u8220\'93}scientific{\u8221\'94} understanding. Thus \softline
while setting some particular within a  {\u8220\'93}comparative perspective{\u8221\'94} may be helpful in stimulating \softline
scientific curiosity and understanding, it does not seem }{\plain \fs24 \ul necessary}{\plain \fs24  to achieve such ends. Likening \softline
Portuguese to Indo-European may stir the imagination in various ways, much as likening the \softline
Native American {\u8216\'91}Indian{\u8217\'92} to the Asian Indian did for the early explorers. But merely in putting \softline
Portuguese into {\u8216\'91}comparative perspective{\u8217\'92} with Indo-European neither entails the process of \softline
making or testing an hypothesis, nor does it guarantee that one can only understand {\u8211\'96} in the \softline
profoundest way {\u8211\'96} the grammar, say, of Portuguese by comparing it to that of Indo-European.  A \softline
{\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative studies would require both the statement of an }{\plain \fs24 \ul hypothesis to be \softline
tested}{\plain \fs24  or a defensible claim about the scientifically consequential common class membership of \softline
the two languages.  \par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab Thanks, however, to the sciences of linguistics, archeology and history, we can embark on \softline
hypothesis-testing, assignment of class membership and even the search for causes. Thanks to \softline
knowledge provided by Indo-European studies, we can both test hypotheses concerning \softline
Portuguese and Indo-European, as well as make the case that they are members of a real class of \softline
languages, and that the ancient formation of Indo-European }{\plain \fs24 \ul caused}{\plain \fs24  Portuguese in part to be what \softline
it is. We can, for example, make sense of gaps or apparent anomalies in languages to create or \softline
identify missing forms by {\u8220\'93}interpolating a graph{\u8221\'94} as it were, as Marc Bloch put it.(Bloch 1967, p. \softline
47) I do not know Portuguese well enough to offer an example, but one from English may serve. \softline
What explains {\u8211\'96} }{\plain \fs24 \ul causes}{\plain \fs24  {\u8211\'96} the occurrence of relatively unvoiced {\u8220\'93}gh{\u8221\'94} in the pronunciation of \softline
modern English of words like {\u8220\'93}enough{\u8221\'94} or {\u8220\'93}knight{\u8221\'94}? What moreover explains {\u8211\'96} }{\plain \fs24 \ul causes}{\plain \fs24  {\u8211\'96} how \softline
these words may have been pronounced in earlier forms of English, such as Chaucerian Anglo-Saxon, when the study of this earlier literature {\u8211\'96} say its poetics {\u8211\'96} reveals that such words rhyme \softline
with words ending in a}{\plain \fs24 \ul  hard}{\plain \fs24  {\u8220\'93}gh{\u8221\'94}? If, because of knowledge of the history of the English-speaking peoples and their relation to Germanic speakers,  we assume a comparative perspective \softline
with German, earlier or modern, the answer becomes clear. Even though we have no direct \softline
knowledge of Anglo-Saxon, we can advance the hypothesis that {\u8220\'93}knight{\u8221\'94} was pronounced more \softline
or less like the German {\u8220\'93}}{\plain \fs24 \ul genug}{\plain \fs24 {\u8221\'94} and {\u8220\'93}}{\plain \fs24 \ul Knecht}{\plain \fs24 {\u8221\'94} {\u8211\'96} which as it happens means roughly the same as \softline
{\u8220\'93}enough{\u8221\'94} and {\u8220\'93}knight,{\u8221\'94} respectively. Such an example from comparative historical phonology, \softline
for example, provides a good model of what a successful, scientific program of comparison {\u8211\'96}  \softline
{\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative studies  {\u8211\'96} one in which hypotheses can be tested, causes cited, \softline
and classes of cultural entity constructed.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab If then we are to obtain more than informal acquaintances with things, say with a \softline
language, we will need to know (or decide)  what the language resembles and what it does not \softline
resemble.  We would need to know (or decide) to what }{\plain \fs24 \ul class}{\plain \fs24  (or classes) the language belongs \softline
and to what class (or classes) it does not belong {\u8211\'96} at least if we are to be said to have something \softline
more than mere acquaintance with the language and thus something we might describe as \softline
{\u8220\'93}scientific{\u8221\'94} knowledge.  Thus, knowing in a scientific sense what Portuguese is, }{\plain \fs24 \ul necessarily}{\plain \fs24  \softline
requires knowing, for instance, that it }{\plain \fs24 \ul belongs}{\plain \fs24  (or }{\plain \fs24 \ul can be said }{\plain \fs24 to belong) to the class of Indo-European languages, and }{\plain \fs24 \ul not}{\plain \fs24  to the class of, say, Semitic languages.  Of course, Portuguese may \softline
be said to belong at the same time to other classes of language as well as it belongs to the Indo-European family.  But, this does not count against my claim that class membership and \softline
knowledge of it would be necessary for one to claim scientific knowledge of Portuguese.  By \softline
extension, I am saying that at least part of what is true of the claim to have scientific knowledge \softline
of language is true of other features of culture: if {\u8220\'93}comparative perspective{\u8221\'94} is }{\plain \fs24 \ul necessary}{\plain \fs24  here, it \softline
is }{\plain \fs24 \ul necessary}{\plain \fs24  elsewhere.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab Whatever the merits and outcome of this particular testing and hypothesizing strategy, it \softline
should be clear that hypothesis testing would }{\plain \fs24 \ul require}{\plain \fs24  comparison and in what ways it might \softline
require such testing.  In the kind of concrete contexts students of culture and history typically \softline
work, hypothesis testing would, moreover, require }{\plain \fs24 \ul concrete}{\plain \fs24  comparative perspectives. This is to \softline
say that the necessity of comparison, at the level where hypotheses are being tested would not \softline
simply be }{\plain \fs24 \ul logically}{\plain \fs24  or conceptually necessary about making up the class of Indo-European \softline
languages. It is a }{\plain \fs24 \ul matter of historical fact}{\plain \fs24  that the members of that class of languages are what \softline
they are.  Therefore, the necessity of testing hypotheses about English comparatively over against \softline
other members of the Indo-European family is what one may call {\u8220\'93}material{\u8221\'94} necessity.  In the \softline
context of this article, one expects that in many cases, matters of fact will figure in approaching \softline
the questions one raises.  Therefore, beyond the {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} of comparison having a logical \softline
or conceptual necessity, it also has a }{\plain \fs24 \ul material}{\plain \fs24  one.  Typically, this comes out in such \softline
modifications of comparison as evidenced in enterprises that traffic in matters of material fact, \softline
such as {\u8220\'93}}{\plain \fs24 \ul cross cultural}{\plain \fs24  comparison{\u8221\'94} or {\u8220\'93}comparative }{\plain \fs24 \ul history,}{\plain \fs24 {\u8221\'94} as opposed, say, to comparative \softline
}{\plain \fs24 \ul philosophy}{\plain \fs24 . Examples of leading thinkers who have in fact practiced and theorized a {\u8220\'93}strong \softline
program{\u8221\'94} of comparative studies would be the Durkheimian {\u8220\'93}nucleus{\u8221\'94} of Durkheim, Hubert and \softline
Mauss, and a series of followers in the Durkheimian tradition {\u8211\'96} Louis Dumont, Antoine Meillet, \softline
Georges Dum{\f2\u233\'e9}zil, Marc Bloch and Lucien Febvre. But, no mention of a  {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in \softline
comparative studies can exclude thinkers in other intellectual worlds such as Max Weber and the \softline
linguists Roman Jakobson or Paul Thieme. \par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab Goethe{\u8217\'92}s classic formulation about knowledge of languages expresses well the spirit of \softline
the {\u8220\'93}strong program.{\u8221\'94} {\u8220\'93}He, who knows one language, knows none.{\u8221\'94} In 1873, this slogan was \softline
adopted by the great study of religion, Friedrich Max M{\f2\u252\'fc}ller, to apply equally to our \softline
understanding of other cultural areas, like religion and mythology.  More recently Andr{\f2\u233\'e9} K{\f2\u246\'f6}bben \softline
gave voice to the essence of what a {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} would be, here applied to comparative \softline
ethnology: {\u8220\'93}Without knowledge of other societies, I do not even know where to look or what to \softline
look for.{\u8221\'94}}{\plain \fs24 (K{\f2\u246\'f6}bben 1970, p. 593)}{\plain \fs24  What each of these formulae assert is that comparison is \softline
}{\plain \fs24 \ul necessary}{\plain \fs24  for understanding or explaining religion. In this sense, this, along with the consequent \softline
effort at testing hypotheses, would articulate the essence of what a {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} would be. \par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab }{\plain \fs24 \par
}{\pntext\pard {\'95}\tab}
