{\plain \fs24 \i Natural Comparisons: Durkheim{\'92}s Empiricism and Residual Evolutionism }{\plain \fs24 \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 Having said what I mean by a  {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative studies, I want to \softline
establish two final points. First, Durkheim and his {\u8216\'91}team{\u8217\'92} were chiefly responsible for this new \softline
conceptualization of a  {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative studies, even though they may not have \softline
proven to be its most consistent or successful practitioners. (I note that the Weberian tradition \softline
could as well demonstrate many of the same virtues and strategies as the Durkheimian (Smelser \softline
1976, Ch.3).)}{\plain \fs24  Of historical note is that one of the leading figures in the comparative historical 
phonology of Indo-European, pioneering the kinds of comparisons I related in earlier examples \softline
of Portuguese, Indo-European and such, was Antoine Meillet {\u8211\'96} a person profoundly influenced \softline
by the Durkheimians.}{\plain \fs24  Second, }{\plain \fs24 the Durkheimian {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} was constructed on  \softline
epistemological bases }{\plain \fs24 \ul only partly}{\plain \fs24  acceptable to {\u8216\'91}us{\u8217\'92}. They grounded their comparative method on \softline
}{\plain \fs24 \ul objectivist, evolutionist grounds}{\plain \fs24 , although, as I shall show, they were gradually moving away \softline
form these positions to one more acceptable to {\u8216\'91}us.{\u8217\'92} }{\plain \fs24 Let me open a small parenthesis to say what \softline
I think is entailed in this {\u8216\'91}us{\u8217\'92} before moving on to the significance of what the Durkheimians did \softline
to establish a  {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative studies.\par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 Regarding }{\plain \fs24 \i evolution}{\plain \fs24 , {\*\bkmkstart I_32_take_32_it}I take it{\*\bkmkend I_32_take_32_it} for granted that evolutionist views of culture and society {\u8211\'96} \softline
except perhaps for technology {\u8211\'96} no longer have the kind of status}{\plain \fs24 \ul  for {\u8216\'91}us{\u8217\'92} }{\plain \fs24 that was taken for \softline
granted by leading thinkers of the 19}{\plain \fs24 \super th}{\plain \fs24  century, such as the Frazers, Tylors and such. Religions, \softline
for example,  are not ranked according to their degree of }{\plain \fs24 \ul evolution}{\plain \fs24  {\u8211\'96} at least not without very \softline
considerable contesting. The religions of the Inuit, for example, are just }{\plain \fs24 \ul different}{\plain \fs24  from, say, High \softline
Church Anglicanism. To make the claim of their being different because one is more }{\plain \fs24 \ul evolved}{\plain \fs24  \softline
than another would involve one in a perhaps interminable controversy of contestation. Because of \softline
the way he regarded the religions of aboriginal Australia, to name only one set of examples, \softline
Durkheim must also be included among the evolutionists although, as we will see, he seems a \softline
genuine transitional figure.\par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 Regarding }{\plain \fs24 \i objectivity,}{\plain \fs24  I also take for granted that the idea of the constructed character of \softline
technical terms and objects likewise need not extensively be argued here }{\plain \fs24 \ul for {\u8216\'91}us{\u8217\'92}}{\plain \fs24 . When we study \softline
{\u8216\'91}myth{\u8217\'92} or {\u8216\'91}totemism{\u8217\'92} or {\u8216\'91}sacrifice{\u8217\'92} and so on, for example, {\u8216\'91}our{\u8217\'92} epistemological principles \softline
dictate that we ask what is meant by these terms. {\u8216\'91}We{\u8217\'92} feel that it is necessary to take \softline
responsibility for our concepts. This is so in part because term, like {\u8216\'91}myth{\u8217\'92} or {\u8216\'91}totemism{\u8217\'92} or \softline
{\u8216\'91}sacrifice{\u8217\'92} and so on have a history of being contested as to their conceptual natures. Moreover, \softline
in our social sciences, although we use these terms as part of our own language, we often do not \softline
appreciate that the same terms might not be recognized in the societies to which we apply them. \softline
Their application is therefore part of what {\u8216\'91}we{\u8217\'92} as investigators do, and not necessarily part of \softline
how the folk we study live. Malinowski was attracted to {\u8216\'91}}{\plain \fs24 \i lili{\u8217\'92}u{\u8217\'92} {\u8211\'96} }{\plain \fs24  a kind of story that his native \softline
informants related to him as being important stories. Immediately, Malinowski decided to call \softline
such stories {\u8216\'91}myths,{\u8217\'92} for better or for worse. The point to be made here is not that we should \softline
never }{\plain \fs24 \i translate}{\plain \fs24  in this way, but merely {\u8211\'96} or at the very least {\u8211\'96} not forget that we are doing so, and \softline
that therefore that {\u8216\'91}}{\plain \fs24 \i lili{\u8217\'92}u{\u8217\'92} }{\plain \fs24 and {\u8216\'91}myth{\u8217\'92} may not map onto each other perfectly. Some little {\u8216\'91}edge{\u8217\'92} \softline
may stick out of  {\u8216\'91}}{\plain \fs24 \i lili{\u8217\'92}u{\u8217\'92} }{\plain \fs24 that {\u8216\'91}myth{\u8217\'92} fails to cover, for example.  Intercultural translation of this \softline
sort is inevitable; one only enjoins caution, sophistication and readiness to adapt to cultural \softline
differences.\par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 Back to Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s contribution to comparative studies. Nothing could be clearer than \softline
Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s avowed resolve to put {\u8216\'91}strong{\u8217\'92} comparative study at the center of his entire \softline
sociological enterprise than his claim that {\u8220\'93}Comparative sociology is not a special brand of \softline
sociology, it is sociology itself, insofar as it ceases to be purely descriptive and aspires to account \softline
for facts{\u8221\'94}(Durkheim 1982, p. 157).  There, in }{\plain \fs24 \ul The Rules}{\plain \fs24  in 1894, Durkheim lays down a six-point program for {\u8216\'91}strong{\u8217\'92} comparative studies which seems to have been common property of \softline
the entire Durkheimian group: \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\li1440\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 (1) comparative studies aim to provide ({\u8220\'93}why?{\u8221\'94}) explanations of social \softline
phenomena because they alone can provide the {\u8220\'93}indirect experiments{\u8221\'94} necessary 
to test hypotheses;\par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\li1440\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 (2) such {\u8220\'93}indirect experiments{\u8221\'94} need to be carried out ({\u8220\'93}how?{\u8221\'94}) within the context \softline
of the {\u8220\'93}total social fact{\u8221\'94}, thus \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\li1440\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 (3) requiring intense studies of, \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\li1440\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 (4) well-chosen cases, \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\li1440\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 (5) of roughly the same order of development, \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\li1440\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 (6) In this way ({\u8220\'93}so what?{\u8221\'94}) Durkheim believed we would be able to trace the \softline
historical development of institutions, and thus to understand our own situation \softline
better(Durkheim 1982, ch. 6). \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 Why, however, does this set of proposals for comparative study count as a call for a \softline
{\u8220\'93}strong{\u8221\'94} program in comparative studies, and from whence does its {\u8220\'93}strength{\u8221\'94} derive. To what \softline
extent, if at all, does the strength of this program derive from sources other than those \softline
empowering evolutionist {\u8220\'93}comparative method{\u8221\'94} {\u8211\'96} even as Durkheim indicates that he believes it \softline
does (as in point (5)? \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 Overall, Durkheim believes the {\u8220\'93}strength{\u8221\'94} of his comparative program is evident from the \softline
fact that he believes it would eventuate in sociological {\u8220\'93}laws{\u8221\'94}  {\u8211\'96} the ultimate test of success in \softline
hypothesis-testing, the discovery of {\u8216\'91}causes.{\u8217\'92}(Durkheim 1982, p. 153) These laws were for him \softline
assured by the objective reality of the evolutionary process of the development of societies that \softline
Durkheim assumed from his earliest works, such as the }{\plain \fs24 \ul Division of Labor in Society}{\plain \fs24  and the \softline
developmental classification of societies found there.(Durkheim 1984) Even in his late work, \softline
such as in }{\plain \fs24 \ul Elementary Forms of the Religious Life}{\plain \fs24 , Durkheim identifies certain phenomena to be \softline
compared within well-drawn {\u8220\'93}primitive{\u8221\'94} contexts (e.g. aboriginal Australia), by comparing the \softline
{\u8216\'91}same{\u8217\'92} phenomena within another well-drawn {\u8220\'93}primitive{\u8221\'94} contexts, (e.g. native North America, \softline
(points 3, 4 above), in order to test a hypothesis of {\u8220\'93}concomitant variation{\u8221\'94}(Durkheim 1982, pp. \softline
151-3) (Point 1 above) {\u8211\'96} to discern laws or law-like relationships.  {\u8220\'93}As soon as we have proved \softline
that in a certain number of cases, two phenomena vary with each other, we may be certain we are \softline
confronted with a law,{\u8221\'94} he notes emphatically.(Durkheim 1982, p. 153)   Mauss later identified \softline
this method of Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s with Darwin{\u8217\'92}s method of doing experiment governed by the \softline
certainties of the evolutionary process, and therefore as a method with avowed scientific \softline
intentions. This, Mauss described as the method of {\u8220\'93}indirect experiment{\u8221\'94} {\u8211\'96} }{\plain \fs24 \i indirect}{\plain \fs24  hypothesis-testing, since like Darwin and matte of the causal genesis or demise of species and their members \softline
long since vanished, the social scientist lacked the ability to perform {\u8220\'93}live{\u8221\'94} experiments {\u8211\'96} \softline
directly to test theses regarding many sorts of human subjects.(Karady 1974, pp. 170-1) \softline
Durkheim would therefore have found congenial what Radcliffe-Brown said many years later \softline
about the role of comparison in the making of a scientific endeavor: {\u8220\'93}without systematic \softline
comparative studies, anthropology will become only historiography and ethnography,{\u8221\'94} Radcliffe-Brown asserted.(Radcliffe-Brown 1958, p. 110)  For Durkheim, sociology was more indeed, and \softline
partly so because developmental schemes guaranteed the existence of certain objective historical \softline
processes that provided a framework in which to evaluate and select data.\par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 Now, although we may not require or expect the objective evolutionary process or \softline
the{\u8220\'93}laws{\u8221\'94} both Durkheim and Radcliffe-Brown did, many of us interested in promoting the \softline
scientific aims of the study of religion  would be pleased if the study of religion would do a good \softline
deal more hypothesis testing or searching for the }{\plain \fs24 \i causes}{\plain \fs24  of religious behavior and thought. So, 
while the Durkheimians represent a tradition in which comparative studies aim to produce \softline
necessary truths ({\u8220\'93}laws{\u8221\'94}) by the necessary application of  comparison, others may be satisfied \softline
with the use of comparative studies for more modest goals, such as serving heuristic purposes or \softline
achieving {\u8216\'91}understanding.{\u8217\'92} \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 Ironically, we can show how comparison aids understanding in this way by showing how \softline
Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative studies can both historically and logically be better \softline
}{\plain \fs24 \ul understood}{\plain \fs24  by seeing it in the {\u8216\'91}comparative perspective{\u8217\'92} of its opposition to a Frazerian \softline
{\u8220\'93}comparative method{\u8221\'94}! Since Frazerian comparative method was arguably as {\u8220\'93}strong{\u8221\'94} {\u8211\'96} in \softline
intention, at least {\u8211\'96} as Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s was supposed to be, what were Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s objections to it \softline
and proposed improvements upon it? I believe they are two. First, {\u8220\'93}comparative method{\u8221\'94} lacked \softline
a certain selectivity of cases to be compared, despite the role of evolution in its justification for \softline
making certain selections. Comparisons seemed extravagant and disorderly. Second, Frazer and \softline
others of his ilk failed fully, from Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s view,  to consider the integrity of social wholes in \softline
determining the meaning of the institutions compared. How do we know the things claimed to be \softline
like each other being compared are really in the class {\u8211\'96} {\u8216\'91}comparable{\u8217\'92} {\u8211\'96} given that they may occur \softline
in different cultural settings? Is the }{\plain \fs24 \i lili{\u8217\'92}u}{\plain \fs24  narrative of the Trobriand Islanders the same as the \softline
}{\plain \fs24 \i mythos}{\plain \fs24  of the Greeks?(Strenski 1987)  \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 How then did Durkheimian comparisons promise to improve on Frazer and company? \softline
Short of the help evolutionism provided the investigator seeking to know what item to select for \softline
comparison, by what }{\plain \fs24 \ul criteria}{\plain \fs24  should things be  }{\plain \fs24 \ul selected}{\plain \fs24  for comparison? The answer seems to lie \softline
in a kind of lazy empiricism.  This makes Durkheim appear naive about the un-constructed \softline
nature of his concepts, such as totemism. As an empiricist and evolutionist, Durkheim felt that \softline
comparisons in general were only as strong as the concepts employed in them. For Durkheim, \softline
only scientific concepts had foundations sufficiently sturdy to endure the rigors of generalization \softline
of cross-cultural comparison.  Thus, part of him fell in with the empiricist confidence in \softline
commonsense. Totemism was just {\u8216\'91}out there{\u8217\'92} because people in different parts of the world \softline
seemed to worship animal and plant species, and to involve them in socially approved rituals. \softline
Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s thought followed the well-known prescription of empiricist or positivist faith in the \softline
power of {\u8216\'91}data{\u8217\'92} to impress themselves upon the receptive mind {\u8211\'96} \par
}\pard \fs24\li1440\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 in order to objective, science must start from sense perceptions and not from \softline
concepts that have been formed independently from it. It is from observable data \softline
that it should derive directly the elements for its initial definition. Moreover, it is \softline
enough to call to mind what the task of scientific work is to understand that \softline
science cannot proceed otherwise.(Durkheim 1982, p. 81)\par
}\pard \fs24\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 Accordingly, since social {\u8216\'91}data{\u8217\'92} are fully objective data {\u8211\'96} {\u8220\'93}things{\u8221\'94} {\u8211\'96} in Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s view, they \softline
too can act to impress themselves upon the mind:\par
}\pard \fs24\li1440\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 social phenomena are things and should be treated as such. To demonstrate this \softline
proposition one does not need to philosophize about their nature or to discuss the \softline
analogies they present with phenomena of a lower order of existence. Suffice to \softline
say that they are the sole datum afforded the sociologist. A thing is in effect all \softline
that is given, all that is offered, or rather forces itself upon our observation. To \softline
treat phenomena as things is to treat them as data, and this constitutes the starting \softline
point for science. Social phenomena unquestionably display this \softline
characteristic.(Durkheim 1982, p. 69)\ul0 \tab \ul0 \tab \ul0 \tab \ul0 \tab \par
}\pard \fs24\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 Durkheim even believed he could guarantee the absolute neutrality and objectivity of the \softline
investigator.  How {\u8220\'93}the facts are classified does not depend on him, or on his own particular cast \softline
of mind, but on the nature of things{\u8221\'94} themselves. (Durkheim 1982, p. 76) \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 \par
}{\pntext\pard {\'95}\tab}
\pard \fs24\s1\li720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlblt\pnstart1\pnindent1\pnhang{\pntxta {\'95}}}

{\plain \fs24 }{\plain \fs24 \i Unnatural Comparisons: Durkheim{\'92}s Rationalism }{\plain \fs24 \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 Now, even as we may disagree with Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s evolutionism and empiricism, one may \softline
agree  with Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s recommendation that {\u8220\'93}comparative studies{\u8221\'94} should proceed with detailed \softline
and well drawn cases (Point 2, 3). After all, why should not data be as rich and exact as possible? \softline
Similarly, why would one quarrel with the proposition that items should be }{\plain \fs24 \ul selected}{\plain \fs24  with care \softline
(Point 4) and, assuming one wished to pursue a {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative studies, for the \softline
purpose of }{\plain \fs24 \ul testing hypotheses}{\plain \fs24  (Point 1)? \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 The issue of {\u8216\'91}selection,{\u8217\'92} provoked by Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s empiricism, however, still remains \softline
problematic. Have we  {\u8216\'91}selected{\u8217\'92} the }{\plain \fs24 \i same phenomena }{\plain \fs24 or not? Durkheim was convinced, for \softline
example,  that Australian {\u8216\'91}totemism{\u8217\'92}  makes a better kind of sense because it is set alongside \softline
North American {\u8216\'91}totemism,{\u8217\'92} and had certain hypotheses tested against it. One can agree readily.  \softline
Indeed, it would seem necessary in order to understand Australia {\u8216\'91}totemism{\u8217\'92} that we see it within \softline
the context of other {\u8216\'91}totemisms.{\u8217\'92} But, since {\u8216\'91}totemism{\u8217\'92} is not a natural kind, a species with its \softline
name written on it, how do we know that we really have the same phenomenon {\u8211\'96} {\u8216\'91}totemism{\u8217\'92} {\u8211\'96} in \softline
different cases?  \par
}\sect \sectd \sbknone\marglsxn1260\pgndec\headery1440\footery1440\endnhere\endnhere 
