\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 I believe that Durkheim and his group answer this puzzle by concluding {\u8211\'96} while not fully \softline
admitting it to themselves {\u8211\'96} in effect, that it is }{\plain \fs24 \ul they}{\plain \fs24  who decide what totemism is, and what its \softline
relatives are. An act of conceptualizing }{\plain \fs24 \ul will}{\plain \fs24  comes surprisingly into play among thinkers who \softline
would in all likelihood refuse to admit it. This radical shift in epistemology of concept formation \softline
from a {\u8216\'91}lazy empiricism{\u8217\'92} to an actively theorizing rationalism is what makes Durkheim vexing and \softline
interesting at the same time is his confusion on this point. Durkheim thus {\u8216\'91}solves{\u8217\'92} this dilemma by \softline
contradicting his own extreme empiricism or positivism, and becoming {\u8211\'96} in part at any rate {\u8211\'96} \softline
critical of the status of commonsense notions and their value for science. Durkheim tells us, that \softline
the example of Copernicus taught him  how unreliable a science based on (the {\u8220\'93}illusions{\u8221\'94} of) \softline
commonsense would be and, therefore, how conceptual revolution and reform were required for \softline
the formation of scientific concepts fit for cross-cultural comparison.(Durkheim 1982, p. 61) \softline
Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s emphasis upon rationalist conceptual construction produced many original and \softline
influential works of cross-cultural comparison devised to establish certain sociologistic hypotheses \softline
about the nature of concepts such as }{\plain \fs24 {\u8220\'93}magic{\u8221\'94}, {\u8220\'93}religion{\u8221\'94}, {\u8220\'93}sacrifice{\u8221\'94}, and so on. \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 Hubert and Mauss{\u8217\'92}s  }{\plain \fs24 \ul Sacrifice}{\plain \fs24 , for example,  does not {\u8220\'93}discover{\u8221\'94} a reality waiting {\u8220\'93}out \softline
there{\u8221\'94} in nature, ripe for the finding. It }{\plain \fs24 \ul recommends}{\plain \fs24  a certain use of the word, {\u8220\'93}sacrifice{\u8221\'94}; it \softline
consists of a sustained theoretical argument {\u8211\'96} all made within a comparative context {\u8211\'96} for a \softline
particularly Durkheimian use of the concept, {\u8220\'93}sacrifice.{\u8221\'94}  }{\plain \fs24 \ul Sacrifice}{\plain \fs24  then represents a fundamental \softline
theoretical demonstration of the Durkheimian  group{\u8217\'92}s {\u8220\'93}strong{\u8221\'94} comparative work. An hypothesis \softline
about the theoretical identity a term is advanced and tested in comparative contexts {\u8211\'96} not on the \softline
basis of the concept{\u8217\'92}s identity being inscribed in nature, but rather in terms of how well it served \softline
the needs of explanation for the Durkheimians themselves!(Strenski 1998; Strenski 2003)}{\plain \fs24  \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 But Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s venture into this new epistemological territory did not necessarily begin \softline
auspiciously nor proceed steadily. Durkheim never really abandoned an appeal to the object \softline
character of cultural and social evolution to solve the problem of how to select things for 
comparison. In the }{\plain \fs24 \ul Elementary Forms of the Religious Life }{\plain \fs24 , he notoriously appeals to the \softline
evolutionist notion of  a {\u8216\'91}simple{\u8217\'92} society as the appropriate area in which the investigator should \softline
search. \par
}\pard \fs24\fi720\li1440\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 Without plunging too deeply into the study of facts, it is not difficult to summarize \softline
in what area to look for the characteristic properties of social types.... \par
}\pard \fs24\li1440\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 If therefore we knew the simplest society that ever existed, in order to make our \softline
classification we should only have to follow the way in which these simple societies \softline
joined together and how these new composites also combined.(Durkheim 1982, p. \softline
111-2)\par
}\pard \fs24\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 As noted earlier in our list of Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s {\u8216\'91}6 points{\u8217\'92} in the concluding paragraph of the }{\plain \fs24 \ul Rules}{\plain \fs24 ,  \softline
Durkheim says. {\u8220\'93}The comparison can therefore only serve as proof if we can eliminate this \softline
disturbing factor of the age of a society.  To do this, it will be sufficient to consider the societies \softline
which one is comparing at the same period of their development.{\u8221\'94}(Durkheim 1982, p. 158)\par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab How and why Durkheim began to change is still somewhat a mystery. Both empiricist and \softline
rationalist tendencies may have been there all along. Parsons and others have argued that  \softline
Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s thought {\u8220\'93}strained{\u8221\'94} towards theoretical positions opposed to a positivist theory of \softline
action. Thomas Gieryn observes that this vacillation in Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s attitude to the place of interests \softline
in theory construction meant that Durkheim both believed that facts were selected according to \softline
human interests but that their truth was not relative to human interests.(Gieryn 1982, pp. 122-3) \softline
Even though we may never know the answer to the reasons for this epistemological change, we at \softline
least can comprehend what it was, and how that set comparison on a new footing. In their new \softline
mood, the Durkheimians acknowledged the role of creativity and will in comparison. The \softline
{\u8220\'93}strength{\u8221\'94} then of this newly justified Durkheimian  {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative studies came \softline
from the fruitfulness of the interpretive virtuosity and theoretical imagination of the researcher or \softline
research programs that launched the comparisons in question.\par
