\pard \fs24\s1\fi-720\li720\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlblt\pnstart1\pnindent1\pnhang{\pntxta {\'95}}}

{\plain \fs24 }{\plain \fs24 \i Durkheim{\'92}s Worthy Successors {\'96} The {\'93}Annales{\'94}}{\plain \fs24 \par
}\pard \fs24\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 \tab One of the surprising results of this chapter in the history of science is that the foremost  \softline
practitioners of this new {\u8220\'93}critical{\u8221\'94}, imaginative and interpretive comparative methodology sketched \softline
by Durkheim and his team were the comparative historians of the }{\plain \fs24 \ul Annales}{\plain \fs24  School, Marc Bloch, \softline
Lucien Febvre, even down to our contemporary, Ferdinand Braudel. They owed much to the \softline
Durkheimians, as did other notable practitioners of Durkheimian comparative method, such as \softline
linguist Antoine Meillet and historian, Henri Berr.(Walker 1963-4; Walker 1980; William H. \softline
Sewell 1967)  They achieved this not only by virtue of Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s example, but also because of \softline
their assimilation of German critiques positivism led by such historically-minded neo-Kantian \softline
idealist philosophers such as Rickert, and also by the hermeneutics of Wilhelm Dilthey. Bloch had \softline
studied both in Dilthey{\u8217\'92}s Berlin and Wundt{\u8217\'92}s Leipzig from 1908-9, and was well placed by his \softline
location in Strasbourg to absorb the intellectual influences from Germany.(Craig 1983; Perrin \softline
1948, pp. 162-3)  Bloch and his collaborators realized this new style of comparative history by \softline
means of several methodological innovations not utterly foreign to the Durkheimians, but not \softline
embraced consistently by them either. These are the role of interpretation or hermeneutics in \softline
knowledge, the place theory and problematic with respect to data, and the sense in which \softline
comparisons could and should be disciplined in order to achieve maximum effect, and thus \softline
{\u8216\'91}strength.{\u8217\'92}  \par
}{\plain \fs24 \ul0 \tab The end result of the conception of comparative history achieved by the }{\plain \fs24 \i Annalistes}{\plain \fs24  was to \softline
create, firstly,  {\u8220\'93}comparative{\u8221\'94} historical studies that drew their {\u8216\'91}strength,{\u8217\'92} in part at least, from the \softline
explanatory fruitfulness of the }{\plain \fs24 \ul interpretive}{\plain \fs24  process in history.  In their magisterial article {\u8220\'93}History{\u8221\'94} \softline
in the }{\plain \fs24 \ul Encyclopedia of the Social Sciences }{\plain \fs24 (1932), Henri Berr and Lucien Febvre develop this \softline
proper and essential role of {\u8220\'93}imagination{\u8221\'94} {\u8211\'96} of subjective factors {\u8211\'96} in history writing. {\u8220\'93}While \softline
physical phenomena would be known even without the intervention of someone to describe and \softline
classify them, the historical past is only known to the extent that there is an image of it {\u8211\'96} in other \softline
words, to the extent that it is created by the mind.{\u8221\'94}(Berr & Febvre 1932, p. 357) Against any \softline
positivist {\u8220\'93}objectivism{\u8221\'94} Berr and Febvre say clearly, {\u8220\'93}The historical facts is by no means always \softline
{\u8216\'91}given.{\u8217\'92} Very often the historian must in some degree create it with the aid of hypotheses and \softline
conjectures, by delicate impassioned labor.{\u8221\'94} (Berr & Febvre 1932, p. 363) As a result, it is not \softline
{\u8220\'93}facts{\u8221\'94} that autonomously determine research but the }{\plain \fs24 \i {\u8220\'93}selection{\u8221\'94} }{\plain \fs24 of facts. And, in this {\u8220\'93}selectivity{\u8221\'94} \softline
even the natural sciences partake. Once more, Berr and Febvre: {\u8220\'93}It is to pretend that the histologist, \softline
has only to put his eye to the lens of a microscope to discover immediately definite facts which can \softline
be utilized as they are, while in truth the}{\plain \fs24 \i  essential portion of his labor consists in creating and \softline
interpreting the objects of his observation }{\plain \fs24 with the aid of singularly complicated techniques.{\u8221\'94} (Berr \softline
& Febvre 1932, p. 363) }{\plain \fs24 (My emphasis) For the }{\plain \fs24 \i Annalistes}{\plain \fs24 , then, interpretation was essential to the \softline
task of doing history, comparative or not.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab Secondly, Bloch also made a {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative studies possible by \softline
introducing another methodological feature, half-heartedly embraced by Durkheim, but not \softline
followed consistently. Bloch unambiguously gave priority to theory and problematic over facts and, \softline
second, synthesis to analysis.  Where Durkheim wanted things both ways, and often thought on \softline
balance that facts spoke for themselves, Bloch was a more outspoken rationalist. Bloch urged the \softline
investigator to mistrust factitious arrangement of things in the world. His oft quoted maxim puts \softline
this view succinctly: {\u8220\'93}The unity of place is only disorder. Only the unity of the problem creates a \softline
center.{\u8221\'94}(Bloch 1934, p. 81) His earlier 1928 classic essay on the comparative study of European \softline
societies fleshes out his vision that brings to fruition implicit tendencies in the Durkheimian \softline
projects for conceptual reform in sociology of religion. Bloch here boldly strides across a boundary \softline
where the Durkheimians lingered.\par
}\pard \fs24\li1440\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 But before phenomena can be interpreted, they must be discovered. And this \softline
preliminary step will reveal in the first place the usefulness of the comparative \softline
method. But {\u8211\'96}  it may be asked {\u8211\'96} is it really necessary to go to such trouble to \softline
{\u8216\'91}discover{\u8217\'92} historical facts? They are and can only be known through documents: in \softline
order to bring them to light, isn{\u8217\'92}t it enough to read texts and monuments? Yes, but \softline
}{\plain \fs24 \i one must know how to read them}{\plain \fs24 . A document is a witness; and like most witnesses, \softline
it does not say much except under cross-examination. }{\plain \fs24 \i The real difficulty lies in \softline
putting the right questions}{\plain \fs24 . That is where comparisons can be of such valuable help \softline
to the historian, who is always in the position of the magistrate hearing the \softline
case.(Bloch 1967, p. 48) (My emphases. See also confirmation in(Walker 1963-4, \softline
pp. 247-8)\par
}\pard \fs24\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont}
{\plain \fs24 \par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab Thirdly, we know that Bloch was as dubious about Frazerian  {\u8220\'93}comparative method{\u8221\'94} as was \softline
Durkheim. (Rhodes 1978, p. 47)}{\plain \fs24  Accordingly, Bloch interpreted Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s view of how to make \softline
comparisons {\u8220\'93}strong{\u8221\'94} in recognizably Durkheimian, and thus anti-Frazerian {\u8211\'96}  ways. }{\plain \fs24 Recall that 
Durkheim claimed that six requirements should govern comparisons {\u8211\'96} if they were to be {\u8216\'91}strong.{\u8217\'92} \softline
Of these six, 3-5 were affirmed by the }{\plain \fs24 \i Annalistes}{\plain \fs24  and reflect the common critique of Frazer that \softline
they shared with the Durkheimians. These were numbers 3-5: the third point required }{\plain \fs24 \i intense \softline
}{\plain \fs24 indeed focused and detailed studies, such as Durkheim undertook in the}{\plain \fs24 \ul  Elementary Forms of the \softline
Religious Life}{\plain \fs24 . The fourth called for (4) }{\plain \fs24 \i well-chosen}{\plain \fs24  cases, again, such as of Australia that \softline
Durkheim chose because of his (misguided) evolutionist views. The fifth point likewise limited \softline
comparisons in terms of the historical relations between items to be compared {\u8211\'96} in Durkheim{\u8217\'92}s \softline
evolutionist parlance as {\u8220\'93}of roughly the same order of development.{\u8221\'94} Jettisoning evolutionism, to \softline
Bloch these points meant that comparisons had to be properly controlled and well drawn. \softline
Explicitly citing his opposition to the sprawling comparisons of a Frazer, Bloch says that {\u8220\'93}to \softline
compare various... societies...(one ought to compare) societies that live close to one another, and \softline
that go back to one common origin, at any rate to several.{\u8221\'94} (Bloch 1967, p. 48)  As the comparative \softline
work of Indo-Europeanists showed, comparison between and among closely related societies held \softline
more promise for powerful results than Frazer was able to achieve. Bloch argued that his more \softline
focused, Durkheimian method of comparison {\u8211\'96} {\u8220\'93}the one with the more limited horizon is also the \softline
richer in results. Because it is more capable of rigorous classification, and more critical about the \softline
objects it compares, it may hope to reach conclusions of fact that are less hypothetical and much \softline
more precise.{\u8221\'94}(Bloch 1967, p. 48)  \par
}{\plain \fs24 \tab In this little sketch of history and epistemology of comparative studies  I have tried to show \softline
how comparative studies have been variously conceived, and more importantly how a powerful and \softline
modern conception of comparative studies struggled to emerge among the Durkheimians and those \softline
influenced by them. The study of religion might do well to apply the methods and rationales of the  \softline
{\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative studies conceived by the anti-positivist Durkheimians and the \softline
}{\plain \fs24 \i Annalistes}{\plain \fs24 . Their epistemological sophistication, reflecting the role of the investigator as well as \softline
the constructed nature of {\u8216\'91}things{\u8217\'92} compared, are bracingly contemporary. As such, they provide an \softline
epistemologically plausible way of doing comparison in the {\u8216\'91}strong{\u8217\'92} sense, and in doing so, to \softline
advance the study of religion towards scientific, or at the very least more rigorous, research goals. \softline
The ability of a {\u8220\'93}strong program{\u8221\'94} in comparative studies to move the study of religion toward a \softline
more rigorous practice of positing and testing hypotheses makes this kind of comparison well \softline
worth doing.\par
